  world leaders show up and how would you  go about persuading them to attend  well i think it's very very worrying  that that is happening and that you've  got world leaders not not potentially  not coming look i'm afraid to say the  government hasn't taken this summit with  nearly the seriousness that it deserves  and we've seen a series of missteps from  the government cutting overseas aid  which has made vulnerable countries  think that we are not serious about  helping them to make the transition not  having a green recovery here at home  telling other countries to power past  coal but flirting with a new coal mine  ourselves i'm afraid time is very short  and this is a summit in peril now what  does the government needs to need to do  it needs to deliver for vulnerable  countries that ten years ago we promised  to help those vulnerable countries make  the adaptation to climate change we've  still not delivered the finance they  need to deliver on that they need to  have no quarter with with giving a free  pass to countries like australia if  they're not going to face up to their  climate commitments we need to say to  them you've got to face up to their  climate commitments we've just done a  trade deal with them where we allowed  them to drop their climate commitments  and crucially we've got to deliver a  green recovery here at home the prime  minister has got to take this much much  more seriously we need all the senior  members of government applying  themselves to this in a way they frankly  haven't before you say that and yet they  would say we've got world-beating  targets for getting down our carbon  emissions they would say we've announced  an end to the sale of petrol and diesel  cars by 2030 10 years ahead of schedule  they'll talk about promising to  quadruple the amount of offshore wind  capacity within a decade they would say  they're doing their bit  well what they've done is they've  announced targets which you're right are  internationally very respectable and i  say to them that's a good it's good that  they've done that the problem is there  is a yawning chasm between the targets  and the delivery because they're not  delivering you know take this issue of  retrofitting or insulating homes we're  facing a gas price crisis  what could we have done to have avoided  this if we'd insulated our homes we  could have cut gas demand by something  by up to 25 in people's homes and  created lower bills and lower carbon  emissions but but year after year we've  been saying to government take these  measures these could create jobs  we could  help families and they haven't done that  so so i agree with you actually  internationally we're doing okay in  terms of the targets but we've got to  deliver and the announcement that labour  has made today this investment  announcement is exactly what i hope  government does frankly so that we can  deliver our promises to the british  people on the climate crisis  ed miliband thanks very much indeed  thank you
 

